      {
        "ballot_id": "B031",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "For the invention and development of optical lattice clocks."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B006",
    "B009"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Optical lattice clocks are a major, durable advance in precision measurement: they have repeatedly reached extraordinary accuracy in independent implementations and enable new tests of fundamental physics. Katori and Ye made complementary contributions that established the field, giving the achievement a clear experimental basis and a plausible two-person attribution.",
    "case_against": "The clock advance developed over time, and the boundary between the original invention and later improvements that enabled present performance is not simple. The citation must recognize the foundational achievement without implying that two people account for all subsequent progress.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "He ranked horizon-scale black-hole imaging first, arguing that it made black holes resolved astrophysical structures and that later observations strengthen the result, while noting the difficulty of assigning credit across a large collaboration.",
        "response": "I still regard the imaging as a remarkable observational achievement, but the distinction between revealing structure and testing gravity should be kept precise: the images show expected black-hole-scale structure, and the gravity test is not yet the clearest part of the case. The collaboration scale also makes individual attribution harder than for the clock work."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "She described the first black-hole image and later observations as strong evidence, while stressing that the result came from a large international collaboration.",
        "response": "The continuing observations strengthen the scientific result, though ongoing development of imaging methods also makes it important to separate the original achievement from later refinement. That concern reinforces my preference for the clock achievement, whose independent implementations and complementary contributions by Katori and Ye are easier to identify."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "He argued that Katori and Ye independently developed leading clock approaches, while noting that the boundary between invention and later precision advances needs care.",
        "response": "I agree that their complementary foundational contributions support the proposed attribution. The citation should remain focused on the invention and development of the optical lattice clock, with the historical record used to distinguish that work from the broader engineering advances that followed."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The historical boundary between the original clock invention and later advances enabling present performance needs careful review."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
